Setting up the application
-----
git clone https://gitlab.practical-devsecops.training/marudhamaran/cmcp-oauth-mcp-secure.git
cd ~/cmcp-oauth-mcp-secure
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
uv pip install -r requirements.txt

Audit the source files
In auth.py, find the JWT signing key:
====================
grep -n '^SECRET' auth.py

List each hardcoded password in the USERS dictionary:
-==============
grep -n '"password"' auth.py

To read the full USERS block:
==================
sed -n '39,58p' auth.py

In job_board_app.py, check how the Flask session key is set:
=========================
grep -n 'secret_key' job_board_app.py

search in files multiple words and files
=============
grep -n 'admin123\|^SECRET\|secret_key' auth.py job_board_app.py

Install the Vault CLI
=============
export VAULT_VERSION="1.5.0" && wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/vault/${VAULT_VERSION}/vault_${VAULT_VERSION}_linux_amd64.zip && unzip vault_${VAULT_VERSION}_linux_amd64.zip && mv vault /usr/local/bin/


Point the CLI at the lab Vault server:
-----------
export VAULT_ADDR='https://vault-gc4olprd.lab.practical-devsecops.training'

Initialize and unseal Vault
A new Vault server starts sealed. Nothing works until you do two things:

Initialize once. This creates five unseal keys, one root token, and writes encryption setup to the server.
Unseal with any three of those five keys. Until the third key is entered, Vault stays sealed.
Save all init output to a file. You will read unseal keys and the root token from that file in this step and later steps.

Initialize Vault
----------
vault operator init | tee vault-init.txt

unseal key
-----
vault operator unseal $(grep 'Unseal Key 1:' vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')
vault operator unseal $(grep 'Unseal Key 2:' vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')
vault operator unseal $(grep 'Unseal Key 3:' vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')

check status
-----
vault status


Log in and export your token
-------------------
vault login $(grep -m1 'Initial Root Token' vault-init.txt | sed 's/.*:[[:space:]]*//')
vault secrets list

env varibale set
---------------
export VAULT_TOKEN=$(cat ~/.vault-token)
echo $VAULT_ADDR
echo $VAULT_TOKEN | head -c 8

Enable KV v2
=========
vault secrets enable -path=secret kv-v2
vault secrets list

Store application secrets
=================
python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"

Replace FLASK_SECRET with the hex value as below:
----------------------
vault kv put secret/mcp-oauth/jwt signing_key='change-me-use-a-256-bit-secret-in-production'
vault kv put secret/mcp-oauth/users/admin password='admin123'
vault kv put secret/mcp-oauth/users/manager password='manager123'
vault kv put secret/mcp-oauth/users/employee password='employee123'
vault kv put secret/mcp-oauth/flask secret_key='FLASK_SECRET'

verfiy a read
===
vault kv get secret/mcp-oauth/jwt
vault kv get secret/mcp-oauth/users/employee

Install hvac (official python lib for vault)
================
uv pip install hvac==2.4.0

Patch auth.py
=========
sed -i '/^from dotenv import load_dotenv/a import os\nimport hvac as _hvac\n\ndef _vault_kv(path):\n    c = _hvac.Client(url=os.environ["VAULT_ADDR"], token=os.environ["VAULT_TOKEN"])\n    return c.secrets.kv.v2.read_secret_version(path=path, raise_on_deleted_version=True)["data"]["data"]\n\n_jwt   = _vault_kv("mcp-oauth/jwt")\n_users = {u: _vault_kv(f"mcp-oauth/users/{u}") for u in ("admin", "manager", "employee")}' auth.py


Replace the hardcoded SECRET with the value fetched from Vault:
sed -i '/^SECRET    = /c\SECRET    = _jwt["signing_key"]' auth.py

sed -i 's/"password":       "admin123"/"password":       _users["admin"]["password"]/' auth.py
sed -i 's/"password":       "manager123"/"password":       _users["manager"]["password"]/' auth.py
sed -i 's/"password":       "employee123"/"password":       _users["employee"]["password"]/' auth.py

verify changes
---------
grep -n '^SECRET\|"password"\|_vault_kv\|_jwt\|_users' auth.py | head -10

